## One buyer's change of assurance method sits behind a fall in a sustainable-purchasing share

The Global Coffee Platform (GCP), an industry membership group, counts how much green coffee large buyers purchase through sustainability programs it recognizes. Daily Coffee News reports that 14 roasters and retailers bought about 69% that way in 2025, down from 75%. Recognized volume fell 8.9% while total purchases fell under 1%, so the market did not shrink; less of it passed through recognized programs. GCP says the decline was largely influenced by one major company's sourcing shift. [[1]](https://dailycoffeenews.com/2026/11/10/gcp-reports-drop-in-sustainable-purchasing-among-large-green-coffee-buyers/)

That company is JDE Peet's. Its share on GCP's measure fell from 72.94% to 47.04% after it moved from certification- and verification-based sourcing to a risk-based human rights and environmental due diligence approach, under which it reports 88.8% of its coffee responsibly sourced, a figure the article neither defines nor verifies. Much of the overall decline came in second-party programs, the in-house verification schemes of roasters and traders or tailored ones from groups such as Enveritas, which fell 26%, while independent certification such as Fairtrade or Rainforest Alliance held steady. The article does not say how much of that 26% is JDE Peet's. [[1]](https://dailycoffeenews.com/2026/11/10/gcp-reports-drop-in-sustainable-purchasing-among-large-green-coffee-buyers/)

The two JDE Peet's figures measure different things: 47.04% counts coffee bought through programs GCP recognizes, and 88.8% is the company's own account under a different method. So a fall from 75% to 69% fits a buyer that changed how it assures its sourcing and fits one that buys less responsibly sourced coffee, and nothing captured separates them. The panel and the list of eligible programs also changed (14 reporters, 26 to 30 programs), so the two years are not measured on the same base. Until JDE Peet's volume by program type and an independent check of the 88.8% are available, the 69% can be cited as the share of purchases flowing through recognized programs, not as a reading of sourcing practice. [[1]](https://dailycoffeenews.com/2026/11/10/gcp-reports-drop-in-sustainable-purchasing-among-large-green-coffee-buyers/)

